# Next.js Rules (App Router)

## General

- Use Next.js App Router conventions.
- Prefer Server Components by default.
- Add `"use client"` only for local state, event handlers, browser APIs, or animations.
- Do not mix client/server logic unnecessarily.

## File structure

- Keep `page.tsx` and `layout.tsx` clean and minimal.
- Do not place heavy business logic inside route files.
- Extract logic into `features/`, `lib/`, or hooks.
- Add `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, and `not-found.tsx` for user-facing routes when appropriate.

## Data fetching

- Fetch on the server whenever possible.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side fetching.
- Keep fetching logic separate from UI when appropriate.
- Always handle loading, error, and empty states.

## Routing

- Use clean, semantic route structures.
- Avoid deeply nested routes without clear purpose.

## Forms and actions

- Use typed forms and Server Actions.
- Validate on the server; never trust client-only validation.
- Keep server actions small: validate → business logic → format response.
- Return typed, predictable shapes.

## Images and metadata

- Use `next/image` for content images with meaningful `alt` text and appropriate `sizes`.
- Use `generateMetadata` for public pages (title, description, OG when relevant).

## Performance and caching

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders and heavy client-side logic.
- Do not prematurely optimize.
- Be intentional about `fetch` caching vs `no-store` — document non-obvious cache choices.

## Security

- Never expose secrets to the client.
- Sanitize or escape user-generated HTML.
- Use `.env.example` for required environment variables.
